https://arxiv.org/pdf/1503.02531

Distilling the Knowledge in a Neural Network

The problem with ensembles
- Combining many models usually improves performance.
- But running 10 to 20 or more models at inference time is vry expensive.
- So the paper basically asks if we can compress the knowledge of an ensemble into one smaller model.

Also, the author says that knowledge can be viewed as a mapping between input to ouputs.
So, a student model doesnt need the teachers architecture or paramters, it can learn the teachers behaviour.

In this paper, soft targets is the core idea.
Normal labels : cats = 1, all others = 0
A teachers produces something like cat = 0.7, dog = 0.2 etc
So small probabilities contain more information about which classes considers similar.

The student learns the teacher's generalization
- This is deeper than simply copying predictions.
- The teacher's output distribution captures how it tends to make mistakes.
- The student therefore learns some of the teacher's learned structure/generalization behavior.

Temparature also plays an important role.
- Normal softmax has T = 1.
- Increasing T makes the PD softer.
- IT exposes relationship between classes.

Using both Soft and hard targets.
- The students learns from the teachers soft predictions.
- Its own hard targets.

Temperature isn't simply "higher is better"
- When the student has enough capacity, a broad range of temperatures can work.
- When the student is much smaller, intermediate temperatures can work substantially better.
- In their MNIST experiment, a 30-unit-per-layer student performed best around T=2.5–4.

A small model can recover surprisingly much of a large model's knowledge
MNIST:
- Large model: 67 errors
- Small model trained normally: 146 errors
- Small model distilled from teacher: 74 errors

